WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.682
Welcome to Module 2 of the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path:

2
00:00:03.682 --> 00:00:05.800
the boundary and least privilege.

3
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.438
Before any call is made, you need to know
which actions run from a vendor's cloud,

4
00:00:10.438 --> 00:00:12.975
and which non-person identities make them.

5
00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:17.563
Then each identity has to hold exactly
what its calls need, and no more.

6
00:00:17.563 --> 00:00:20.407
This module follows the guide's Gate 1,

7
00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:23.987
and the developer workbook's
Chapters 02 and 03.

8
00:00:23.987 --> 00:00:27.774
You'll confirm the boundary on
paper, learn the ServiceNow roles,

9
00:00:27.774 --> 00:00:32.774
SailPoint scopes and Graph permissions each
kit task needs, and then make privilege

10
00:00:32.774 --> 00:00:37.840
something a test can check, by catching
three kinds of over-wide grant on a laptop.

11
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:42.740
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.302
Because the easy fix is the wrong one.

13
00:00:45.302 --> 00:00:48.706
An administrator who grants
the itil role to make

14
00:00:48.706 --> 00:00:52.191
it work has given the
integration every ITSM table.

15
00:00:52.191 --> 00:00:56.878
An API client with a manage scope can
change accounts if its token is stolen.

16
00:00:56.878 --> 00:00:59.632
One integration user
for everything means the

17
00:00:59.632 --> 00:01:02.740
account that reads evidence
can also close findings.

18
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.367
And when a client gains
a call nobody listed,

19
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:09.015
someone grants a role in production
to make the error go away.

20
00:01:09.015 --> 00:01:13.440
Each of those has a guard in the kit, and
you'll use every one of them in the lab.

21
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.955
The idea of the module is simple: confirm
the boundary on paper first, then make

22
00:01:17.955 --> 00:01:22.900
privilege something a test can check,
instead of something someone remembers.

23
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.502
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

24
00:01:29.502 --> 00:01:31.512
which takes about an hour with the lab.

25
00:01:31.512 --> 00:01:34.811
You'll explain what Gate 1
records about each product's

26
00:01:34.811 --> 00:01:38.549
authorization and each action
that runs from a vendor's cloud.

27
00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:43.212
You'll describe the ServiceNow roles the
kit uses and why they're split by task.

28
00:01:43.212 --> 00:01:47.650
You'll describe the SailPoint scopes
and Graph permissions each task needs,

29
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.538
and why the workflow scope
gets a client of its own.

30
00:01:50.538 --> 00:01:53.626
And you'll verify a grant
against the kit's call list,

31
00:01:53.626 --> 00:01:55.538
and catch a grant that's too wide.

32
00:01:55.538 --> 00:01:57.288
You need Module 1 first.

33
00:01:57.288 --> 00:02:02.638
For the Graph rows, the Microsoft Automation
path's first module covers permissions.

34
00:02:02.638 --> 00:02:08.850
Lab 1 runs on fixtures; Lab 2 needs a
developer instance and a sandbox tenant.

35
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.575
Gate 1 records and decides;
it builds nothing.

36
00:02:15.575 --> 00:02:16.925
It has six steps.

37
00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:22.393
One: confirm each product's FedRAMP status
on the Marketplace on the day you decide,

38
00:02:22.393 --> 00:02:26.413
and record the listing, its impact
level, its path and the date.

39
00:02:26.413 --> 00:02:29.400
Two: confirm what's
inside the authorization.

40
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.183
SailPoint doesn't publish a list of
features missing from its authorized

41
00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:39.044
environment, so ask in writing whether
Workflows, Native Change Detection

42
00:02:39.044 --> 00:02:43.513
and the workflow HTTP Request step,
which Gates 3 to 6 rely on, are there.

43
00:02:43.513 --> 00:02:47.262
Three: confirm the product you
bought is the one authorized.

44
00:02:47.262 --> 00:02:50.762
Four: record which actions
run from a vendor's cloud.

45
00:02:50.762 --> 00:02:54.599
Five: confirm the apps are in
ServiceNow's government Store.

46
00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:57.658
And six: register the
integration identities,

47
00:02:57.658 --> 00:03:02.670
with a certificate or federated
credential, never a long-lived secret.

48
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.458
Four non-person identities
carry the integration.

49
00:03:09.458 --> 00:03:13.757
SailPoint's Entra connector app
registration in the agency's tenant.

50
00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:16.483
The ServiceNow Entra
spoke's app registration.

51
00:03:16.483 --> 00:03:20.892
The ServiceNow integration user
that SailPoint's workflow posts as,

52
00:03:20.892 --> 00:03:25.533
together with the verifier account
the re-read client closes records as.

53
00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:29.258
And the SailPoint API client
the kit's clients read with.

54
00:03:29.258 --> 00:03:32.143
As design guidance, each
has two named owners

55
00:03:32.143 --> 00:03:35.107
and a federal sponsor in
the system inventory.

56
00:03:35.107 --> 00:03:36.271
Now the flows.

57
00:03:36.271 --> 00:03:40.842
SailPoint's cloud connectors call
Microsoft Graph from SailPoint's cloud;

58
00:03:40.842 --> 00:03:45.708
only the Active Directory path runs
through its on-premises virtual appliance.

59
00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:50.682
And, by inference, its workflows call
out from SailPoint's cloud to ServiceNow.

60
00:03:50.682 --> 00:03:55.738
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
15 prefers execution through a connector

61
00:03:55.738 --> 00:04:00.650
inside the boundary, so each vendor-cloud
action becomes a recorded data flow.

62
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.862
Here's how the kit makes
privilege checkable.

63
00:04:06.862 --> 00:04:11.894
A file called calls dot JSON lists
every HTTP call a Python client makes:

64
00:04:11.894 --> 00:04:15.440
the system, the method and
path, the tasks that make it,

65
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.563
the least privilege it needs,
and the vendor page that says so.

66
00:04:19.563 --> 00:04:23.132
A test runs the clients
against a fake HTTP session

67
00:04:23.132 --> 00:04:26.276
and fails on any call
the file doesn't declare.

68
00:04:26.276 --> 00:04:29.693
So a new call fails the test
until someone adds its row,

69
00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:34.587
its privilege and its source, and a
reviewer sees that row in the pull request.

70
00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:37.974
The test also asserts that
no task asks SailPoint

71
00:04:37.974 --> 00:04:41.526
or Graph for a write scope,
except workflow creation.

72
00:04:41.526 --> 00:04:43.950
Then the kit calls tool reads the file.

73
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.963
Give it a task and it
lists what that task needs.

74
00:04:46.963 --> 00:04:52.720
Give it a grant and it compares, and
exits one on anything missing or extra.

75
00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:01.757
On ServiceNow, the roles are custom roles
in the scoped application, split by task.

76
00:05:01.757 --> 00:05:06.555
That's design guidance: ServiceNow says
the calling user needs roles that allow

77
00:05:06.555 --> 00:05:11.133
access to the table, and no single role
allows adding attachments by default.

78
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:14.495
The finding writer can create
findings, and nothing else.

79
00:05:14.495 --> 00:05:19.108
The verifier can read and write findings
and attach evidence, so it can close them,

80
00:05:19.108 --> 00:05:23.032
which is why the business rule accepts
a close only from that account.

81
00:05:23.032 --> 00:05:27.296
And the evidence reader reads, but can't
change a record it's about to hash.

82
00:05:27.296 --> 00:05:31.508
Each gets its own integration user
and client-credentials client.

83
00:05:31.508 --> 00:05:36.186
The client itself takes a token from a
vault first, then client credentials,

84
00:05:36.186 --> 00:05:39.657
and basic authentication only
on a developer instance.

85
00:05:39.657 --> 00:05:44.860
It pages, retries, and refuses
a secret in its config file.

86
00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.422
Now SailPoint and Graph.

87
00:05:50.422 --> 00:05:56.148
The drift and re-read clients need read
scopes only: search read, and accounts read.

88
00:05:56.148 --> 00:05:59.550
The Accounts endpoints would
accept the manage scope too,

89
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:04.086
so the call list names the read scope
and the comparison flags manage as extra.

90
00:06:04.086 --> 00:06:07.994
Creating SailPoint's workflows
needs the workflow manage scope,

91
00:06:07.994 --> 00:06:10.310
and no client in the kit makes that call.

92
00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:14.919
So, as design guidance, that scope
belongs to a separate client,

93
00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:19.347
used once, by hand or by a reviewed
pipeline, and then removed.

94
00:06:19.347 --> 00:06:24.133
On Graph, the app registration needs
User dot Read dot All, as an application

95
00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:28.686
permission, and AuditLog dot Read dot
All only if the run reads the audit.

96
00:06:28.686 --> 00:06:34.820
Nothing in Pass 1 needs write access to
Entra ID, Active Directory or SailPoint.

97
00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:43.720
One thing a client-credentials token
can't do in SailPoint: act as a user.

98
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.751
It has no user context, and
SailPoint warns that calling

99
00:06:47.751 --> 00:06:51.520
most admin APIs with one
often returns four oh three.

100
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.907
It doesn't say which.

101
00:06:52.907 --> 00:06:56.839
So if Search or Accounts returns
four oh three in your sandbox,

102
00:06:56.839 --> 00:07:00.077
use a personal access token
for a dedicated user at

103
00:07:00.077 --> 00:07:03.469
the lowest user level that
works, and record which one.

104
00:07:03.469 --> 00:07:07.245
Never an administrator's, or
the kit runs as org admin.

105
00:07:07.245 --> 00:07:11.495
Whether a client-credentials token
gets data is on the verify list.

106
00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:15.818
The client pages Search past ten
thousand results with search after,

107
00:07:15.818 --> 00:07:19.458
sorted on I D, and reads accounts
two hundred fifty at a time.

108
00:07:19.458 --> 00:07:22.702
For Graph, it signs in
with a certificate first,

109
00:07:22.702 --> 00:07:26.820
and the cloud setting is
Global for worldwide and GCC.

110
00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.295
Try it.

111
00:07:31.295 --> 00:07:35.145
Part A of Lab 1 runs in the
kit's folder against fixtures.

112
00:07:35.145 --> 00:07:39.358
Step one proves every call is
declared: nine tests pass.

113
00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:43.101
Step two lists what the re-read
client needs on ServiceNow:

114
00:07:43.101 --> 00:07:47.078
a client-credentials token, then
read and write on the finding

115
00:07:47.078 --> 00:07:51.133
table and create on attachments,
all from one role, the verifier.

116
00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:57.008
Step three is the classic mistake: an
administrator gave the verifier itil as well.

117
00:07:57.008 --> 00:08:00.719
The tool lists the calls,
then reports nothing missing,

118
00:08:00.719 --> 00:08:03.771
itil extra, and least
privilege does not hold.

119
00:08:03.771 --> 00:08:04.895
It exits one.

120
00:08:04.895 --> 00:08:09.296
Step four catches the evidence
export reusing the verifier's role:

121
00:08:09.296 --> 00:08:13.057
the evidence reader is missing
and the verifier is extra.

122
00:08:13.057 --> 00:08:16.996
With the verifier's role, the
export could also close findings.

123
00:08:16.996 --> 00:08:22.110
Step five reruns the original
ServiceNow client tests: seven pass.

124
00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.347
Part B turns to SailPoint and Graph.

125
00:08:28.347 --> 00:08:32.160
Step six runs the SailPoint
client tests: four pass.

126
00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.901
Step seven lists what the drift
client needs from SailPoint:

127
00:08:35.901 --> 00:08:37.772
search read and accounts read.

128
00:08:37.772 --> 00:08:41.281
Step eight catches a manage
scope on that read client:

129
00:08:41.281 --> 00:08:44.948
nothing missing, accounts
manage extra, and it exits one.

130
00:08:44.948 --> 00:08:49.009
Step nine checks the drift client's
scopes against the workflow task.

131
00:08:49.009 --> 00:08:52.910
The workflow scope is missing,
and both read scopes are extra.

132
00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:56.622
That's the point of the step:
the two jobs need two clients.

133
00:08:56.622 --> 00:09:00.047
Step ten checks the Graph
app registration twice.

134
00:09:00.047 --> 00:09:05.122
For a drift run that reads the audit,
AuditLog dot Read dot All is missing.

135
00:09:05.122 --> 00:09:09.343
With the required-only option, for
a run that doesn't read the audit,

136
00:09:09.343 --> 00:09:12.910
least privilege holds, and
the command exits zero.

137
00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.010
Lab 2 builds the real
thing in test instances.

138
00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:25.035
In your ServiceNow developer instance,
from Module 1, you create the three custom

139
00:09:25.035 --> 00:09:29.835
roles, one integration user and one
client-credentials integration per role.

140
00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:32.779
Then you compare each user's
roles with the call list,

141
00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:35.723
and confirm the evidence
reader can't patch a record.

142
00:09:35.723 --> 00:09:40.578
In a SailPoint sandbox tenant, you create
a reader API client and a separate

143
00:09:40.578 --> 00:09:44.885
workflow client, compare each with
the call list, and try the reader.

144
00:09:44.885 --> 00:09:48.673
How an agency gets a sandbox
is between it and SailPoint.

145
00:09:48.673 --> 00:09:53.891
Back it out before you leave: delete
the OAuth integrations, users and roles,

146
00:09:53.891 --> 00:09:55.743
and revoke any saved token;

147
00:09:55.743 --> 00:10:00.372
then delete both API clients and
anything under the output folder.

148
00:10:00.372 --> 00:10:02.890
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

149
00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.315
What does the boundary look
like on the Marketplace?

150
00:10:09.315 --> 00:10:13.929
Read on November 1, 2026:
ServiceNow's Government Community

151
00:10:13.929 --> 00:10:17.790
Cloud is FedRAMP certified
at High, on the JAB path.

152
00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:21.460
ServiceNow's commercial cloud
has no listing of its own,

153
00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.215
and some agencies run on it.

154
00:10:23.215 --> 00:10:28.403
SailPoint Identity Security Cloud is
certified at Moderate, on the agency path.

155
00:10:28.403 --> 00:10:33.264
And IdentityIQ, the self-hosted
product, has no listing of its own.

156
00:10:33.264 --> 00:10:36.692
The interim path: on
ServiceNow's commercial cloud,

157
00:10:36.692 --> 00:10:42.486
the authorizing official decides whether
identity tasks may run there before a move,

158
00:10:42.486 --> 00:10:46.077
and, as design guidance,
payloads stay to identifiers.

159
00:10:46.077 --> 00:10:51.120
Where a feature's presence in SailPoint's
authorized environment is unconfirmed,

160
00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.450
build Gates 3 to 6 in the sandbox and hold
production until SailPoint confirms it.

161
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:01.587
The federal requirement.

162
00:11:01.587 --> 00:11:06.075
The guide maps Gate 1 to NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three.

163
00:11:06.075 --> 00:11:11.122
S A nine, external system services, and
FedRAMP authorization: each service's

164
00:11:11.122 --> 00:11:13.045
authorization is confirmed,

165
00:11:13.045 --> 00:11:17.050
or the gap is recorded as a risk
for the authorizing official.

166
00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.612
C A three, information
exchange: each flow is recorded.

167
00:11:21.612 --> 00:11:22.869
I A five and A C six:

168
00:11:22.869 --> 00:11:27.899
the integration identities use
certificates and least privilege.

169
00:11:27.899 --> 00:11:32.499
The chapters map the call list to A C
six, A C two for account management,

170
00:11:32.499 --> 00:11:36.615
A C five because the account that
closes findings is separate,

171
00:11:36.615 --> 00:11:41.537
and C M three because a change to what
the integration may call is reviewed.

172
00:11:41.537 --> 00:11:46.120
The evidence an ISSO keeps: Gate
1's record, plus the call list,

173
00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:51.260
its test, and the comparison
output with each access approval.

174
00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.510
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

175
00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:03.001
First: why does the evidence export
get its own evidence reader role,

176
00:12:03.001 --> 00:12:05.247
instead of reusing the verifier's?

177
00:12:05.247 --> 00:12:06.472
The answer is b.

178
00:12:06.472 --> 00:12:10.410
With the verifier's role, the
export could also close findings.

179
00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:13.110
The reader can't change a
record it's about to hash.

180
00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:14.835
Roles are split by task.

181
00:12:14.835 --> 00:12:19.723
Second: Gate 1 asks you to record which
actions run from a vendor's cloud.

182
00:12:19.723 --> 00:12:20.410
Why?

183
00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:21.374
The answer is a.

184
00:12:21.374 --> 00:12:25.872
Book 15 prefers execution through
a connector inside the boundary,

185
00:12:25.872 --> 00:12:29.648
so each vendor-cloud action
becomes a recorded data flow.

186
00:12:29.648 --> 00:12:34.910
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

187
00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:39.485
To sum up.

188
00:12:39.485 --> 00:12:43.484
You confirmed what Gate 1 records:
each product's FedRAMP status,

189
00:12:43.484 --> 00:12:45.521
what's inside the authorization,

190
00:12:45.521 --> 00:12:49.973
which actions run from a vendor's
cloud, and the integration identities.

191
00:12:49.973 --> 00:12:51.711
And you saw that it builds nothing.

192
00:12:51.711 --> 00:12:56.171
You learned the ServiceNow roles,
SailPoint scopes and Graph permissions

193
00:12:56.171 --> 00:13:00.272
each kit task needs, and why the
workflow scope gets a client of its own.

194
00:13:00.272 --> 00:13:04.166
And on a laptop you caught
three kinds of over-wide grant:

195
00:13:04.166 --> 00:13:08.398
an extra role, a reused role, and
a manage scope on a read client.

196
00:13:08.398 --> 00:13:13.623
Before you ship, work through the
Chapter 02 and Chapter 03 checklists.

197
00:13:13.623 --> 00:13:16.359
Next is Module 3, one
writer per attribute:

198
00:13:16.359 --> 00:13:21.588
Gate 2's decisions about who writes each
attribute and who approves each class,

199
00:13:21.588 --> 00:13:25.100
and a check that finds an
attribute with two writers.
